.\" zshrsall.1 — comprehensive reference for zshrs (parallels zshall(1)).
.\" Combines per-topic content (builtins, options, parameters, ZLE,
.\" expansion, completion, modules, conditional/arithmetic/redirection)
.\" plus the zshrs-only layers (daemon, fusevm, AOT, AOP, caches,
.\" parity modes, recorder, worker pool, AOP intercepts).
.TH ZSHRSALL 1 "2026-06-24" "zshrs 0.12.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zshrsall \- the Z shell, Rust compiled implementation (full reference)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zshrs
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ argument
\&...]
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the comprehensive reference for
.BR zshrs (1).
The short option summary lives in
.BR zshrs (1);
daemon administration in
.BR zd (1).
.PP
.B zshrs
is a strict 1:1 port of the Z shell
.RB ( zsh (1))
into Rust, plus a non-port
.I extensions
layer that adds features zsh does not have. The runtime is a
four-crate workspace:
.IP \fBzshrs\fR 8
the runtime binary
.IP \fBcompsys\fR 8
completion-system subcrate
.IP \fBzshrs-daemon\fR 8
singleton coordinator
.IP \fBzshrs-recorder\fR 8
optional shell-session recorder
.PP
.B src/ported/
holds the strict 1:1 ports (89 files; every function maps to a real
.IB Src/ x .c
zsh function, enforced by
.BR tests/port_purity.rs ).
.B src/extensions/
holds zshrs-only code (31 files: AOT, daemon coordination, plugin/
script/autoload caches, fish-style autosuggestions/abbreviations/
syntax highlighting, the persistent worker pool, ZWC bytecode helpers).
.SH "THE COMPILED PIPELINE"
Every command zshrs runs traverses this pipeline:
.IP \fBlex\fR 8
.IR src/ported/lex.rs ,
matches
.IR Src/lex.c .
.IP \fBparse\fR 8
.IR src/ported/parse.rs ,
matches
.IR Src/parse.c .
.IP \fBemit\fR 8
wordcode emission, then
.B fusevm
opcode lowering.
.IP \fBcompile\fR 8
.B fusevm
JIT-compiles hot units to native machine code on the second
invocation.
.IP \fBrun\fR 8
the bytecode runs in
.B fusevm
with a persistent worker pool servicing builtins and most external
calls without
.BR fork (2).
.PP
The pipeline outputs can be inspected directly via
.BR \-\-dump\-tokens ,
.BR \-\-dump\-ast ,
.BR \-\-dump\-wordcode ,
and
.BR \-\-disasm
(see
.BR zshrs (1)).
Resulting bytecode is cached as rkyv shards under
.IR ~/.cache/zshrs/ .
.SH INVOCATION
.B zshrs
accepts the same argument grammar as
.BR zsh (1).
A login shell sources login files; an interactive shell sources
.IR .zshrc ;
.B \-c
takes a command literal;
.B \-s
forces stdin reading; bare-script invocation
.RB ( "zshrs " script )
runs the script with
.B $0
set to its path. See
.BR zshrs (1)
for the full flag table.
.SH "PARITY MODES"
.TP
.B \-\-zsh
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/zsh .
All caches disabled, daemon disabled. Used by the parity test corpus
.RB ( tests/zsh_compat_parity_gaps.rs )
to verify byte-for-byte equivalence.
.TP
.B \-\-bash
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/bash .
.TP
.B \-\-ksh
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/ksh
(ksh-93 dialect).
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/sh
(Bourne, dash).
.PP
Parity modes never write the cache, never connect to the daemon, and
never load the extensions layer.
.SH "STARTUP FILES"
Same set, order, and gating as
.BR zsh (1):
.PP
.RS
.IR /etc/zshenv " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zshenv " \(em " /etc/zprofile " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zprofile
(login)
.B \(em
.IR /etc/zshrc " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc
(interactive)
.B \(em
.IR /etc/zlogin " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zlogin
(login)
.B \(em
.IR $ZDOTDIR/.zlogout " \(em " /etc/zlogout
(login shutdown).
.RE
.PP
When
.B $ZDOTDIR
is unset,
.B $HOME
is used. The
.B RCS
and
.B PRIVILEGED
options gate sourcing identically to upstream zsh.
.SH "LANGUAGE OVERVIEW"
.SS "Simple commands"
A simple command is a sequence of optional variable assignments
followed by blank-separated words, with optional redirections
interspersed. The first word is the command name; subsequent words
are its arguments. Builtins and shell functions are tried first;
otherwise the command is searched along
.BR $PATH .
.SS "Compound commands"
.B if
.IR list ;
.B then
.IR list "; [" "elif " list ";\ then " list "; ...] [" "else " list "; ] " fi
.br
.B while
.IR list "; do " list "; done"
.br
.B until
.IR list "; do " list "; done"
.br
.B for
.I name
.RB [ in
.IR word ... ]
.RB ; do
.IR list ;
.B done
.br
.B for
.RB (( [ expr1 ]
.RB ; [ expr2 ]
.RB ; [ expr3 ]
.B )) do
.IR list ;
.B done
.br
.B select
.I name
.RB [ in
.IR word ... ]
.RB ; do
.IR list ;
.B done
.br
.B case
.I word
.B in
.IR pattern ")\ " list ";;\ " ... " esac
.br
.RI ( list )
\(em subshell.
.RI { list ;}
\(em current-shell group.
.PP
.B function
.IR name " {" " list " "}" "
or
.IR name " () {" " list " "}" "
defines a function.
.SS "Pipelines and lists"
.IR pipeline " ::= " "[!] cmd [ |[&] cmd ...]"
.br
.IR list " ::= " "pipeline [ ;|&|&&|||| pipeline ...]"
.PP
.B time
prefix times a pipeline using
.BR $TIMEFMT .
.B !
negates exit status.
.B &
backgrounds.
.B &&
and
.B ||
short-circuit by exit status.
.B |&
pipes both stdout and stderr.
.SH "REDIRECTION"
.TP
.BI < file
stdin from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI > file
stdout to
.IR file
(error if
.B NOCLOBBER
set and file exists).
.TP
.BI >| file
stdout to
.IR file
(force; bypass
.BR NOCLOBBER ).
.TP
.BI >> file
stdout appended.
.TP
.BR << " " WORD
heredoc; input until a line equal to
.IR WORD
(quote WORD to disable expansion;
.B <<-
strips leading tabs).
.TP
.BI <<< word
here-string; feeds the expanded
.I word
as stdin.
.TP
.BI <> file
fd for read+write.
.TP
.BI <& fd " / " >& fd
duplicate fd.
.TP
.B &>
\fIfile\fR \(em redirect both stdout and stderr to
.IR file .
.TP
.B &>>
\fIfile\fR \(em append both stdout and stderr.
.TP
.BI <( list )
process substitution \(em expands to a
.B /dev/fd/N
path whose contents are
.IR list 's
stdout.
.TP
.BI >( list )
process substitution \(em expands to a
.B /dev/fd/N
path whose writes are piped to
.IR list 's
stdin.
.TP
.BI =( list )
process substitution to a temp file.
.PP
A leading integer
.RI ( "2>&1" ", " "3>" file )
selects the fd; the default is stdin for
.B <
and stdout for
.BR > .
.B MULTIOS
duplicates writes when multiple redirections target the same fd.
.SH "EXPANSION ORDER"
zshrs expansion happens in this order, matching upstream zsh:
.IP " 1." 4
.B History
expansion
.RB ( "!!" ", " "!N" ", " "!?str?" ", " "^old^new" ").
Parsed from raw input before any other expansion. Disabled in
non-interactive shells unless
.B BANG_HIST
is set.
.IP " 2." 4
.B Alias
expansion (parse time). Global aliases
.RB ( "alias -g" )
expand at any position; regular aliases only as command words.
.IP " 3." 4
.B Process
substitution
.RB ( <(...) ", " >(...) ", " =(...) ).
.IP " 4." 4
.B Parameter
expansion
.RB ( $name ", " "${name[ind]}" ", " "${name:-default}" ", " "${(P)name}" ", " "${(qV)name}" " ...
\(em see PARAMETER EXPANSION below).
.IP " 5." 4
.B Command
substitution
.RB ( "$(...)" ", " `...` ).
.IP " 6." 4
.B Arithmetic
expansion
.RB ( $((...)) ", " $[...] ).
.IP " 7." 4
.B Brace
expansion
.RB ( {a,b,c} ", " {1..10} ", " {01..10..2} ).
.IP " 8." 4
.B Filename
generation (globbing) and tilde substitution.
.IP " 9." 4
.B Word
splitting on
.B $IFS
(only in unquoted contexts and in
.RB $* / $@
within unquoted form).
.SH "PARAMETER EXPANSION"
.SS "Basic forms"
.TP
.B ${name}
value.
.TP
.BI ${name[i]}
array element
.IR i
(1-based unless
.B KSH_ARRAYS
set).
.TP
.BI ${name[i,j]}
slice from
.I i
to
.IR j .
.TP
.BI ${name:- word }
.IR word
if
.I name
unset or empty.
.TP
.BI ${name:= word }
assign
.I word
to
.I name
if unset/empty, then expand.
.TP
.BI ${name:? word }
abort with
.I word
if unset/empty.
.TP
.BI ${name:+ word }
.IR word
if
.I name
set and non-empty.
.TP
.BI ${name# pattern }
strip shortest matching prefix.
.TP
.BI ${name## pattern }
strip longest matching prefix.
.TP
.BI ${name% pattern }
strip shortest matching suffix.
.TP
.BI ${name%% pattern }
strip longest matching suffix.
.TP
.BI ${name/ pat / repl }
replace first
.I pat
with
.IR repl .
.TP
.BI ${name// pat / repl }
replace all matches.
.TP
.BI ${name:offset}
\fIand\fR
.BI ${name:offset:length}
\(em substring slice (bash-compat).
.SS "Modifier flags (parenthesised prefix)"
Inside
.B ${(\fIflags\fB)name}
each character is a flag:
.TP
.B P
treat
.B name
as containing another variable's name (indirection).
.TP
.B V
emit nonprintable bytes in
.B $'\\x..'
form.
.TP
.B q / qq / qqq / qqqq
quote-escape for re-evaluation in increasing strictness.
.TP
.B Q
remove one level of quoting.
.TP
.B e
re-evaluate the expansion result.
.TP
.B s/X/
split on the literal char
.IR X .
.TP
.B j/X/
join elements with
.IR X .
.TP
.B z
shell-aware tokenisation (lexer-token boundaries).
.TP
.B u
remove duplicate elements (uniq).
.TP
.B o
sort ascending;
.B O
sort descending.
.TP
.B a
reverse element order;
.B A
treat the value as the name of an associative array.
.TP
.B k
list keys of an associative array;
.B v
list values;
.B kv
interleave keys + values.
.TP
.B @
expand each element as a separate word (preserve word boundaries
inside double quotes).
.TP
.B M
keep only the matched portion under
.B #
/
.B %
/
.B /
operators;
.B R
keep the unmatched remainder.
.TP
.B L / U / C
lowercase / uppercase / capitalize.
.TP
.B "##"
the
.B (##)
flag turns
.RB " %"
/
.B #
into greedy variants
.RB ( ## ", " %% ).
.PP
Flags combine:
.B "${(kv)assoc}" ", "  "${(j:\\n:)array}" ", "  "${(@f)\"$(<file)\"}" .
.SH "SPECIAL PARAMETERS"
zshrs honours every special parameter from upstream zsh.
.PP
.B Positional / argv:
.B $#
(arg count),
.B $*
(joined with first IFS char),
.B $@
(separate words),
.B $0
(script name),
.BR $1 \(em $N
(positional args).
.PP
.B Status:
.B $?
(last exit status),
.B $!
(last background PID),
.B $$
(shell PID),
.B $PPID
(parent PID),
.B $UID
.B $GID
.B $EUID
.B $EGID ,
.B $-
(current option letters),
.B $_
(last arg of previous command),
.B $pipestatus
(per-segment exit status of last pipeline).
.PP
.B Shell-version / introspection:
.B $ZSH_VERSION
.B $ZSH_NAME
.B $ZSH_PATCHLEVEL
.B $ZSHRS_VERSION
(zshrs-only),
.B $LINENO
.B $FUNCNAME
.B $funcstack
.B $functrace
.B $funcsourcetrace
.B $funcfiletrace .
.PP
.B Time / random:
